## cli · --help (rc 0)
git.grove.provision boot — bare box to acceptance-grade, in ONE command

usage:
  rhx git.grove.provision boot <name> [--mode plan|apply] [--from N] [--trust verified|replace|tofu|keep] [--within S]

inputs:
  <name>    the grove, as rhx git.grove.list names it      (required)
  --mode    plan names the steps and runs none; apply runs them  (default plan)
  --from    the first step to run, 0-4                      (default 0)
  --trust   how the host key is trusted at step 1           (default verified)
            verified — checked against the box's own report; an unattested key halts
            replace  — verified, and a CHANGED key is re-trusted (a rebuilt box)
            tofu     — the check is skipped: a tailnet first contact, by your word
            keep     — the trust step is not run
  --within  seconds to allow one seat's apply               (default 2700)

examples:
  rhx git.grove.provision boot <name>                                    # plan
  rhx git.grove.provision boot <name> --mode apply                       # the whole boot
  rhx git.grove.provision boot <name> --mode apply --trust tofu          # a tailnet first contact
  rhx git.grove.provision boot <name> --mode apply --from 3 --trust keep # resume at the camper

the steps:
  0. grants  — ask the HUMAN-ONLY credentials first, before any reach
  1. reach   — wake the road (an ec2 tunnel, or the tailnet), trust the host key
               (a tailnet first contact: --trust tofu, by your word)
  2. ground  — push, then ONE apply (the seat WITH sudo goes first)
  3. camper  — push, then ONE apply (the seat that does the work)
  4. gate    — git.grove.provision test, with no command in between

the ORDER is the point: ground converges every box-wide fact, so the
camper's apply asks for no root at all — and step 0 runs first so a
grant only a human can make is asked BEFORE a 45-minute unattended run,
never discovered as a claim at the end of one.
exit 0 = provisioned + gated | 3 = a step failed | 2 = bad input

